            <ArticleTitle>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is associated with the -1055 IL-13 promoter polymorphism.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>436-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>IL-13 is strongly implicated in the development of asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). We previously identified an IL-13 promoter polymorphism (-1055 C to T) that is associated with allergic asthma. We now report an increased frequency of the -1055 T allele in COPD patients compared to healthy controls (P=0.002) and compared to a second control group consisting of smoking individuals with normal lung function (P=0.01). A closely linked IL-13 exon polymorphism is present at normal allelic frequencies (P=0.3 and 0.4, respectively). In addition, we observed a normal distribution of two IL-4 polymorphisms at positions -590 and +33 (P=0.2 and 0.9, respectively). These results could implicate a functional role for the IL-13 promoter polymorphism in the enhanced risk to develop COPD.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
